{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ultrametric inequality ultrametric inequality","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md","fileName":"Ultrametric Inequality.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ultrametric Inequality","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 10:10\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Ultrametric Hierarchy and Information]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ultrametric Hierarchy and Information","datasciencefoundations_geometry.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ultrametric inequality ultrametric inequality","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md","fileName":"Ultrametric Inequality.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ultrametric Inequality","heading":"Ultrametric Inequality","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An ultrametric is a distance satisfying the strong triangle inequality: the distance from x to z is no greater than the larger of the distances from x to y and y to z. It is more restrictive than the ordinary triangle inequality.\n\nFor any three points, the two largest pairwise distances are equal. The corresponding triangle is equilateral or isosceles with a small base. This geometry is why nested cluster relationships can be represented by a rooted tree rather than by arbitrary overlap among neighborhoods.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ultrametric Hierarchy and Information","datasciencefoundations_geometry.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequality.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ultrametric Inequali